00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.480
Explain historical relativism. Why&nbsp;
should/shouldn’t we apply it when evaluating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:19.760
historical figures? In your opinion, what are&nbsp;
some pros and cons to historical relativism?

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.360
Uffff. Philosophy wasn’t my strongest&nbsp;
subject, but I can give my two cents.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:25.680
So first, an extremely oversimplified explanation.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.680
Relativism, is an idea that “right” and&nbsp;
“wrong”, or what is considered “truth”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.520
or just standards of reasoning, are relative,&nbsp;
meaning they differ from person to person, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.880
time period to time period and from culture to&nbsp;
culture. This means that the abstract properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:48.960
of things such as being morally right, or wrong,&nbsp;
or something looking beautiful are relative to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.880
a given framework of assessment like cultural&nbsp;
norms, personal standards, etc. Due to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.520
the meaning of statements like something&nbsp;
being morally right or wrong can only be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.360
understood once the framework of assessment of an&nbsp;
individual or a group that made those statements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:10.960
is defined. In essence you cannot fully understand&nbsp;
an abstract statement by someone, for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.840
“this looks beautiful”, if you do not understand&nbsp;
their framework of assessment by which they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:22.320
assessed that thing to be beautiful. However, you&nbsp;
yourself are also bound by a certain framework of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.600
assessment by which you assess things like right&nbsp;
or wrong or something being beautiful. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.720
framework of assessment could be a combination&nbsp;
of your religious beliefs, cultural upbringing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.160
personal standards, etc. And since everyone&nbsp;
has this unique framework of assessment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.920
relativists argue that there exists no&nbsp;
independent vantage point from which to assess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:50.800
whether something is truly morally “right”, or&nbsp;
“wrong”, or “beautiful” etc. It is all relative.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:54.960
Due to this, in its most extreme form,&nbsp;
relativism can be used to condemn those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.680
who project their own assessment of things&nbsp;
like morality towards acts like genocide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.200
which were conducted by peoples whose frameworks&nbsp;
of assessment completely differed from that of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.160
own and therefore, their assessment of what&nbsp;
is morally right or wrong differed as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.560
Basically, who’s to say something&nbsp;
is morally right or wrong, when such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:20.160
an abstract concept is defined by a person's&nbsp;
unique framework of assessment which by nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.920
has no independent vantage point. After all,&nbsp;
one man's hero is another man's villain.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:29.840
Now relativism, just like any&nbsp;
other kind of philosophical ism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:35.440
has variations and different interpretations&nbsp;
and some of its parts overlap with other isms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:41.120
like subjectivism and contextualism, but all this&nbsp;
is beyond the scope of answering this question.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:45.440
However, I can give my opinion about&nbsp;
relativism being applied to history.&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:49.200
Humans have always had some kind of a&nbsp;
framework of assessment through which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:54.640
they assessed abstract things like morality&nbsp;
or beauty or even love, and they have almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:58.960
always projected their assessments of&nbsp;
these things onto the past and sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.680
even the future. The framework of assessment&nbsp;
through which people assessed things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.920
obviously changed over time and differed&nbsp;
between cultures and individuals.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.240
The two main frameworks of assessment the&nbsp;
historian needs to take into considerations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.800
when writing history are the ones of the people&nbsp;
in the time and area he’s writing about and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.840
the time and area he is from. Also important to&nbsp;
the context are the frameworks of assessment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.360
the people living in between then and now; this is&nbsp;
the historiography part of the historical essay.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.520
What is usually the most contested&nbsp;
issue of all this is that people today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.880
including some historians, try to project&nbsp;
their current assessments of abstract ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:41.680
like what is morally right or wrong, which are&nbsp;
defined by current frameworks of assessments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.360
onto the people in the past, even though,&nbsp;
the people in the past clearly had completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:52.160
different frameworks of assessment by which&nbsp;
they assessed what is morally right and wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.760
Relativists would argue that this projection&nbsp;
is a bad practice because as we have discussed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:03.520
all abstract ideas like morality cannot be judged&nbsp;
because there doesn’t exist an independent vantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.640
point from which to judge them. However, I&nbsp;
would argue that doesn’t matter. People have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:13.440
always had different frameworks of assessments&nbsp;
and they have always projected their assessment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:19.840
of abstract ideas onto the past, why should we be&nbsp;
any different? I am sure there will be people 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:25.120
years from now talking about how our assessments&nbsp;
of morality and beauty were completely wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.680
We as a society, through our current&nbsp;
frameworks of assessment, have decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:35.600
that genocide is morally bad, and as such we&nbsp;
should treat genocide in history as morally bad.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:41.600
With that said, what must be emphasized here, is&nbsp;
that the frameworks of assessment used by a person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:48.160
or a peoples in assessing abstract ideas like&nbsp;
morality should be contextualised by a historian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:53.200
because only then can a more fuller picture of&nbsp;
a historical event be gotten. It is important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:59.600
to understand why a peoples in the past thought it&nbsp;
was morally justifiable to conduct a genocide, or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.440
on a lighter note, why they thought&nbsp;
a certain thing looked beautiful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:08.080
There are plenty of events or people&nbsp;
in the past that we would today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:13.360
unequivocally call “bad” that at the time&nbsp;
might have been completely “normal” or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:19.200
milder than the norm, and I see no reason why&nbsp;
all this cannot be mentioned by a historian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:24.560
Stating that by our standards this person&nbsp;
was “terrible” due to these things but by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:30.080
standards of the time he wasn’t that bad because&nbsp;
of these frameworks of assessment of that time.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.560
Hopefully that answers the&nbsp;
question. I am not really sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:36.960
I myself got lost about half way&nbsp;
through explaining it. Anyways.

