00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:51.280
What books would you recommend about&nbsp;
History? (It can be any period/topic)

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:56.880
The one that comes to mind first, most likely&nbsp;
because I recently read it for my nomad video, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:03.280
The Cambridge History of Early Inner Asia it’s, I&nbsp;
think, the best introduction to the Eurasian Nomad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:08.880
history there is, even though research wise it has&nbsp;
some outdated points, but it’s still really good.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:16.160
For historical books I would always recommend&nbsp;
reading Thucydides, because even though he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:22.000
very dry when compared to his contemporary&nbsp;
Herodotus, he is so analytical that I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:28.000
it's with him that the writing of History truly&nbsp;
started not with Herodotus. Don’t get me wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:33.920
Herodotus was still a good step forward, but he&nbsp;
did play lip service to supernatural events and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:39.360
wrote in a more sensationalized story-like way&nbsp;
which was similar to how history was written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:44.080
before the historical method. Basically&nbsp;
I view Herodotus as the transition from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:49.520
fictionalized story telling of history like&nbsp;
the Iliad to using the historical method for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:54.640
writing history which is what Thucydides did.&nbsp;
So to me Thucydides was the first historian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:59.520
and therefore I think it’s important to read his&nbsp;
work. Plus you’ll notice that some of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:05.360
he talks about are still applicable to this&nbsp;
day even though it was almost 2500 years ago.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.760
Why'd you start, and what helps you keep going?

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:15.520
For this question I had like a two page answer&nbsp;
ready but in reality when I looked through it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:21.360
I realized it was all really boring stuff, so&nbsp;
I am just going to extremely oversimplify it.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:27.120
I started because 1. I wanted to be a YouTuber,&nbsp;
I know that sounds shallow but that was one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:32.800
the reasons nonetheless. 2. Because I just like&nbsp;
history and I have always read history books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:38.160
so making videos about something I like just&nbsp;
sounded like fun. 3. History documentaries on TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:43.120
were getting worse and at that time there weren’t&nbsp;
that many history YouTubers on the platform so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:48.320
I thought I was filling in a nice niche. 4. I&nbsp;
wanted to prove a concept to myself which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:53.440
that history is not a useless major to study and&nbsp;
I can actually make money with it if I don’t go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:58.960
the traditional academic route, which in the end&nbsp;
I actually did go the traditional academic route,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:04.320
but I don’t think I would have gone that route&nbsp;
if I didn’t have YouTube as kind of fall back.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:09.200
Over time a 5th reason emerged for why&nbsp;
I do YouTube and that is “variety”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:13.600
Often as an academic historian you get&nbsp;
stuck in researching a single topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:19.040
in a single time period and sometimes this can&nbsp;
get a bit dull since there is little variety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:24.000
but with YouTube I can pick whatever topic I want&nbsp;
to research about and that is amazing. The variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:29.480
of topics I research into for YouTube really&nbsp;
does help me keep my passion for history as I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:34.800
am not stuck with doing just one specific thing.&nbsp;
Everytime I feel a bit bored with my academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:40.000
work, I can go and research into the Viennese&nbsp;
Coffee House, or the Mexican War of Independence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:45.680
things that I would normally never write about if&nbsp;
I was just doing the traditional medievalist rout.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:51.520
So I guess that explains why I started YouTube and&nbsp;
also why I am continuing doing it. Well, that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:56.880
the little extra money I get from it is also&nbsp;
nice, but don’t be fooled, it’s nothing major,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:02.320
if it was I would be doing YouTube full time,&nbsp;
as it is, I am not, but maybe in the future.

