What are some positives and negatives you&nbsp;
would personally say about each of them?

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:17.360
Since my video topics are all over the&nbsp;
place I rarely use the same book twice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:23.120
Like the books I used for the Maximilian video&nbsp;
are kind of useless for my Early Medieval Nomads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:27.520
video. With that said, during my research&nbsp;
as an early eastern european medievalist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:33.200
there is a book that comes up time and time again&nbsp;
and that is Curta’s The Making of the Slavs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:37.280
and a lot of people have a lot of&nbsp;
strong opinions about this book.

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:40.800
If you never read it, and you’re&nbsp;
interested in early Slavic history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:45.920
I definitely recommend reading it, even if&nbsp;
you already know the book's conclusion ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:51.200
of time and you don’t agree with it, it is still&nbsp;
a remarkable piece of research you should read.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:56.000
The amount of work Curta did to write it&nbsp;
is nothing less than astounding and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:00.720
contains the most complete information&nbsp;
on early Slavic sources out there.

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:06.160
With all that said though, Curta did use&nbsp;
postmodernist and Toronto School ideas for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:12.320
conclusion which many historians, including me,&nbsp;
don’t agree with. He views the Slavic ethnicity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:19.120
as being socially constructed by outside observers&nbsp;
who imparted the Slavic ethnos upon a people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:25.120
did not have an ethnic identity. He expanded on&nbsp;
this point in his follow-up book published very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:31.120
recently where he argued that the Slavic languages&nbsp;
were constructed as a lingua franca by various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:36.560
peoples and they did not originate from a single&nbsp;
proto-Slavic language, which would support the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:41.680
fact that the Slavic ethnicity did not originate&nbsp;
from a single ethnic identity but was rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:48.640
imparted on the quote “Slavic” people. Now I am&nbsp;
not going to get into why I don’t agree with Curta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:55.040
because that would take far too long but in short&nbsp;
it seem to me that Curta’s ideas are an extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.040 --> 00:17:00.880
counterreaction to the prominent nationalistic and&nbsp;
conservative views of the many Eastern European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:06.480
historians which honestly still last in one&nbsp;
way or another to this day in eastern Europe.

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:11.200
But yeah The Making of the Slavs is a very&nbsp;
prominent book and even though I don’t agree with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:17.040
its conclusion, I have to say it is a must read&nbsp;
if you are interested in early Slavic history.

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:21.040
What is one area of history that&nbsp;
you would like to learn more about?

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:24.000
Area of history that I know almost nothing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:27.680
and would like to know more about&nbsp;
is classical and medieval China.

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:32.080
Area of history that I know a fair bit&nbsp;
about but also am aware that there’s still a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:35.680
giant amount of history I don’t&nbsp;
know and would like to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:40.000
is, 19th and early 20th century&nbsp;
Europe, and Latin American history.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.760
Has your general view of history&nbsp;
or worldview changed since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:47.840
you've started doing history youtube?
If so, are there any videos you found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:53.040
especially influential, or did these changes&nbsp;
come about gradually and in the background?

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:59.440
I mean definitely yes to the first part, although,&nbsp;
I would call it more evolved than changed but how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:04.640
much YouTube has to do with all that is hard to&nbsp;
say. Since I started YouTube I lived in three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:10.160
different countries, completed a BA a graduate&nbsp;
degree and a Masters degree, and met people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:15.200
from all walks of life. All this obviously&nbsp;
affected my worldview more than YouTube did.

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:19.840
With that said, YouTube definitely helped me gain&nbsp;
numerous skills and the large amounts of research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:25.200
I have done into a wide variety of topics due&nbsp;
to YouTube definitely affected the way I look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:30.000
at history and the world around me but as I&nbsp;
said I don’t think it was the most influential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.920
part of why my worldview evolved from where it&nbsp;
was 6 years ago. I also don’t think there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:40.640
any specific video I can really pinpoint&nbsp;
to as being the most influential in this.

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:43.680
Which time period are you&nbsp;
hoping to make more videos on?

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:48.240
Well unsurprisingly the early medieval&nbsp;
period. For example, I have been promising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:52.160
a video on the early history of the&nbsp;
Romanians for a very long time now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:56.560
it’s just the topic is so convoluted&nbsp;
I am trying to figure out a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.560 --> 00:19:00.240
present it the most objectively I&nbsp;
can, and that is taking a long time.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:04.640
But yeah, expect more early medieval history&nbsp;
videos. Although, I will never stop making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:09.680
videos on random niche history things that&nbsp;
I get interested in for whatever reason.

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:12.880
Favourite type of cheese?
Well with me being a Slovak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:15.920
who actually loves bryndzove halusky, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:22.080
just HAVE to say my favorite type of cheese is&nbsp;
brindza. My hands are tied on this one, sorry.

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:24.400
What YouTube channels do you watch the most?

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:29.280
Well let's get the easy ones out of the way first.
Jesse Cox, Linus Tech Tips, Techlinked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:33.520
PolyMatter, Nativelang, Veritasium,&nbsp;
RTGame, and Captain Disillusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:37.760
are the ones I have watched the most&nbsp;
lately that aren’t history YouTubers.

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:41.440
When it comes to history YouTubers,&nbsp;
there are many that I am subscribed to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:47.120
I think like almost over 60 or something, but&nbsp;
there’s just not enough time in the world to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:52.000
watch everything they release and in that sense&nbsp;
there are only a couple of history YouTubers whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:57.440
new videos I always watch. These are Jack&nbsp;
Rackam, Cogito, Guthlac, Historia Civilis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:01.280
